#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9bf5ef-0b2f-58e3-a02b-38b0b94d9f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278848} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f417c7-1adf-5968-be5e-7a28cd0f3ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280219} {:answer/id #uuid "c67face3-ea0f-54ec-abf8-31d98bb676cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280331} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1ed97-fd98-5ee9-abbe-fa7c46a9977b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278823} :answer-field/id #uuid "40847485-fda7-51d7-b82e-0e2ddf1864ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "932d87ff-4a97-5b0b-b8d7-da2ba43e7f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162763" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefd0f3a-69c8-5bd3-b062-8e0a896eed06" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Linear Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf225b47-4c19-5217-8990-347551b6707b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372204} {:answer/id #uuid "56197474-e8f7-5ce5-8d46-2baf9e825918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372205} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2340ab-e19e-52ae-b570-eac281dc9ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372206} {:answer/id #uuid "3454ace2-65ba-5ed8-8b19-9b8ae2dca207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372207} {:answer/id #uuid "60ca4825-53f4-5526-a63c-844efa53d479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ca4825-53f4-5526-a63c-844efa53d479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372208} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a272c34-6771-5a9a-abbf-859c9c9ddcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b81c4517-112b-539a-a190-05f9b7ce0159" :question/math-academy-id "q-162774" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=12x+4\\sqrt{6}y\\,$ on the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=6.$" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=6.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=6\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨12,4\\sqrt{6}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12=6\\lambda x \\\\ 4\\sqrt{6}=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{2}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=6$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{4}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{12+12}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =6 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),\\,(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-24\\,$ | $\\,24\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}),$ and its maximum value is $24.$"}]}
